================================== P2  Kummer-path lift visibility mod p^4,  p in {3,5,7,11,13,17} ==================================
Setting: in Z/p^4Z, for units a,b (p ∤ a, p ∤ b), does N=a^p+b^p admit a p-th
root c (any c) and especially a unit root c?  Ground truth = full brute-force
root table of x -> x^p over Z/p^4Z; v_p from the index of the first 1-digit.

Deliberately try a naive descent characterisation and show it FAILS:
  claim:  N (v_p(N)=0) has p-th root mod p^4  <=>  N mod p^2 in Im(x->x^p mod p^2)
  counterexamples (N where N mod p^2 is a pth-power mod p^2 but N is not a
  pth power mod p^4, i.e. a real lift failure):
  p=3:  6 counterexamples, first few:  [9, 18, 36, 45, 63, 72]
  p=5:  24 counterexamples, first few:  [25, 50, 75, 100, 125, 150]
  p=13:  168 counterexamples, first few:  [169, 338, 507, 676, 845, 1014]
  p=17:  288 counterexamples, first few:  [289, 578, 867, 1156, 1445, 1734]
Conclusion: descending only mod p^2 is insufficient; the counting below uses the
full root table.  (Roots: any c  includes the root c in ideal (p) (e.g. p=3, roots of
N=18 are exactly the units:  check p=3 below) as well as roots with v_p(c)>0 (e.g.
p=4? no: c in (p) -> c^p in (p^4)=0, so for units a,b: N=0 is the only zero case).

  p |     |U| | root of zero case c in (p) | c in (p) (any c) | unit-root c (a,b,c all units) | runtime
  3 |      54 |             1 (N=0)  |               486 |               0   (incl p|c: 486) |   0.0s
  5 |     500 |             1 (N=0)  |             2,500 |               0   (incl p|c: 2,500) |   0.0s
  7 |   2,058 |             1 (N=0)  |         1,426,194 |       1,411,788   (incl p|c: 14,406) |   0.0s
 11 |  13,310 |             1 (N=0)  |           146,410 |               0   (incl p|c: 146,410) |   1.8s
 13 |  26,364 |             1 (N=0)  |       116,186,148 |     115,843,416   (incl p|c: 342,732) |   7.2s
 17 |  78,608 |             1 (N=0)  |         1,336,336 |               0   (incl p|c: 1,336,336) |  64.9s

interpretation:  Cex - Cun = # unit-pairs with a^p+b^p == 0 mod p^4  (the only non-unit-root
v_values for units a,b are in ideal (p^4)? no, here a,b units -> a^p+b^p = 0,  or has a unit root. OK
density vs all (Z/p^4)^2 (printed for p=3):
  p=3:  486/6,561 = 0.074074  (any root)    0/6,561 = 0.000000  (unit root)
  Takeaway: even among unit (a,b), a^p+b^p is rarely a pth-power: this is the
  visibility step's numerical reflection -- most trails do not lift to a root.

================================== P3  Frey j-invariant  j = 2^8 (a^(2p)+a^p b^(2p)+b^(2p))^2 / (a^p b^p) ==================================
sympy version 1.14.0
Note: at units (a^p,b^p)=(1,2), no integral Frey solution exists (c^p=1+2^p not an
integral pth-power for odd p) -- no FLT counterexample is constructed here.  This is a
numerical verification comparing the exact 2^k * m / 2^q value of j (obtained from
an integer numerator/denominator ex bitwise-exact) against a float64 evaluation,
surrounding the finite-precision degradation of the round-trip.
